{"kind":"report","text":"No findings. Approved exact 599a55db29c6d23a40a6aa92345a3b8af12550c3 for Mint lane A, implementation request #9474 (1eda3c5e), against all four assigned artifacts, with plans/README.md as primary.\n\nReview 6a979799 is answered. The revocation pass rechecks readable expiry after its bounded repository lookup and immediately before each call, including lookup failure and expiry during an earlier revocation. A mixed batch first settles only records already expired, without lookup or wait, and ends; the next pass revokes the remaining eligible records with the adopted 1-second continuation. Each pass makes at most 20 record changes and one count-summary write. I accept this implementation of the existing bound. My former mixed control required all records to settle in one reconcile call; I changed that control to verify the specified two passes, immediate expired settlement, one summary write per pass, and the exact next due time, rather than retaining that extra single-call requirement. The observation's lookup and listing now share one deadline; no listing starts when the lookup consumes it. The report describes these bounds accurately.\n\nThe two P1 repairs from 0ab6dac3 remain effective: every repository acquisition is bounded, late acquisition starts no provider work, and durable error metadata never includes provider message text, arbitrary names/codes/statuses or scope. The original ownership and recovery guards remain: record and stored wake before each distinct create; actual TTL recomputed and recorded after that wake; late IDs become owed without exposure; claim is synchronous in the owner's transaction; conditional row/state transitions; unknown effects retained despite time, inventory and takeover; unmatched inventory tokens untouched; indexed bounded observation, paging and revocation; exact counts; future times preserved by the overdue continuation.\n\nArchitecture: this implements Artroom's adopted Git-package ownership/storage service under protocol section 32 and its design note. It adds the ledger, exports and Node controls, with no caller change. Room alarm composition, landing/log publication and check-job adoption remain lanes B/C. It preserves the normative contract; no new amendment is needed. Under Gitseq's architecture page this is ordinary application Git content: storage, signed sequencing kernel, host binding and Workroom interpretation are unchanged. Main is 99cc4044 with the approved diagnostics changes, while this candidate is based on b803d210; landing must preserve those subsequent changes.\n\nSecurity and authority: I freshly inspected the effective implementation request/promise, review assignment, governing commission 10fcfe4e, independent ratified design approval ad6cc052 and sealed receipt 867e496c. Planner's effective operator roster is #1 and the durable actor projection grants ratifier authority. The commission explicitly authorizes the contract decision with implementation through separate requests; the separately signed lane A request commissions this bounded additive work. The independently approved design was merged, the governing request is satisfied, and its unretired successor design is effective and not stale. This confirms the four authority-chain facts; no live credential authority is conferred. SQL values remain bound parameters; token text is kept only on the live returned object; shared observations do not imply ownership or settle unknown creates. No unresolved security defect found.\n\nSimplification: retain one ledger API, one observation summary and the expired-only pass. Splitting a mixed batch over the next bounded turn is simpler than a second count write or making expired settlement await the provider. Keep the documented choices, distinct retry records, and indexed ownership lookups. The metadata helper can later move to a lower shared package without introducing a Git-to-Room dependency.\n\nIndependent validation at /tmp/artroom-checker-mint